These standards apply to all SBCSD deputies while on duty, regardless of rank, station, or division assignment. Deputies are expected to present a professional appearance consistent with the department's standards at all times.

<Note>
  Supervisors may inspect uniform and equipment at any point during a shift. A deputy who is out of uniform is corrected before taking further calls.
</Note>

## Uniform Classes

SBCSD uses three uniform classes. The assignment or event determines which class is worn.

<AccordionGroup>
  <Accordion title="Class A — Formal" icon="award">
    Worn for ceremonies, funerals, promotions, awards, formal inspections, and any event where the department is represented publicly.

    **Components**

    - Long-sleeve dress shirt, fully buttoned
    - Necktie
    - Dress trousers
    - Polished dress shoes
    - Full rank insignia, name plate, and badge
    - Awards, ribbons, and commendation bars as earned
    - Campaign hat or dress cover where directed

    **Not worn on patrol.** Class A is a ceremonial uniform and is not appropriate for field duty.
  </Accordion>

  <Accordion title="Class B — Standard Patrol" icon="shield">
    The default duty uniform for patrol operations. Unless directed otherwise, deputies report for shift in Class B.

    **Components**

    - Short or long-sleeve duty shirt
    - Duty trousers
    - Duty boots or polished patrol shoes
    - Badge, name plate, and rank insignia
    - Duty belt with issued equipment
    - Body-worn camera (required — see Equipment below)
    - Patrol vest or radio (required — see Equipment below)

    Class B is the uniform members will see on nearly every shift.
  </Accordion>

  <Accordion title="Class C — Utility / Tactical" icon="crosshair">
    Worn for specialized assignments, training, extended search operations, and where Class B is impractical.

    **Components**

    - Utility or tactical shirt
    - Utility trousers
    - Duty boots
    - Subdued or embroidered SBCSD identification
    - Badge displayed on the vest or chest
    - Body-worn camera (required)

    **Authorization required.** Class C is worn by assignment — SED, K9, Air Operations, or when a supervisor directs it for a specific operation. It is not a personal preference alternative to Class B.
  </Accordion>
</AccordionGroup>

### Identification

Regardless of class, a deputy must be identifiable as SBCSD at all times. Badge, name, and department identification are visible on every uniform class. Plain clothes or unmarked appearance requires a specific investigative assignment and supervisor approval.

